  "id": "Compound::DB00228 - Gene::54207",
  "verbalization": "Enflurane, an organofluorine anesthetic agent, is implicated in the interaction with Kcnk10 through a multi-step mechanism. The evidence suggests that Enflurane binds to Kcnk2, which is a subunit of the Twik-related potassium channel (Trek), and this channel is subsequently participated by Kcnk10. This implies a direct or indirect interaction between Enflurane and Kcnk10 via the Kcnk2-Trek pathway. Additionally, Enflurane is included in the class of general anesthetics, which includes Isoflurane, a compound that binds to Kcnk10. The shared ancestry of Enflurane and Isoflurane in the class of organofluorine anesthetics suggests a potential common mechanism of action, possibly involving the modulation of potassium channels. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Enflurane in modulating Kcnk10 through the activation of the Kcnk2-Trek complex, which may underlie its anesthetic effects.",
